{
  "answer_text": "Require fine‑grained, least‑privilege delegation (scoped tokens/OAuth‑style) with explicit, auditable user consent flows and easy review/revocation. Use short‑lived, session‑bound tokens that can be revoked, separate read vs write/execute privileges, bind tokens to specific clients to prevent replay, and require per‑action confirmation for high‑risk operations. Complement these with tamper‑evident audit logs of model actions, transport and at‑rest encryption, input/output sanitization/redaction, rate limiting/abuse detection, and monitoring/policy enforcement to limit model access to only what the user consented to.",
  "source_quote": "MCP results in more capable AI applications or agents which can access your data and take actions on your behalf when necessary."
}

{
  "answer_text": "Using registry-listed public MCP servers gives fast discovery and easier integration with less hosting and maintenance burden, making them useful for experimentation and rapid onboarding. The tradeoff is reduced control over availability, latency, privacy/security posture, versioning cadence, and SLAs. Hosting a custom/self-hosted MCP server gives full control over authentication, access control, data handling, tooling, and SLAs, but shifts responsibilities to you for designing and operating the server (scaling, monitoring, backups), managing capability discovery and schema/version compatibility, enforcing authz and tenant isolation, sandboxing and vetting tools/resources, and maintaining compliance and upgrade procedures.",
  "source_quote": "Central catalog of publicly available MCP servers for discovery and implementation"
}

{
  "answer_text": "Treat discovered registry entries as untrusted until vetted. Before integrating a server discovered from a public or custom/local registry, an MCP client must enforce endpoint and manifest validation (TLS and signed manifests), capability and schema verification, strong authentication/authorization (short‑lived tokens or mTLS and least‑privilege scopes), runtime sandboxing and execution limits, allowlist/denylist and operator approval flows, comprehensive logging/auditing with revocation/quarantine capability, and explicit user/operator consent before sending PII or other sensitive data. These controls are necessary because registry discovery provides availability but not trust—servers may misbehave, differ in capabilities, or expose sensitive data if not properly constrained.",
  "source_quote": "Central catalog of publicly available MCP servers for discovery and implementation"
}

{
  "answer_text": "If a client or UI assumes the registry will always return the latest-compatible versions or relies on cached server metadata, upgrades or server-side changes can cause runtime breakage (schema/method/auth changes, deserialization errors, missing capabilities) and can silently make pinned or reproducible workloads fail. Clients should explicitly request and validate version identifiers (support semver or explicit tags), expose and allow pinning of specific versions instead of only showing \"latest\", perform schema/capability negotiation and strict validation, use cache TTLs and revalidation tied to registry-provided version IDs, and treat custom endpoints as independent authorities requiring full version and capability checks. These practices preserve reproducibility and provide clear failures or graceful fallbacks when incompatibilities occur.",
  "source_quote": "Show only latest versions"
}

{
  "answer_text": "If the Official MCP Registry or its UI becomes slow or unavailable (for example shows “Loading servers...” or a “Retry” option), a client should expect discovery to be delayed or to fail to return server lists. Resilient strategies include implementing retries with exponential backoff/jitter and a capped retry budget, exposing a user-visible retry option, and using cached or operator‑pinned fallback endpoints to allow degraded or read‑only operation while the registry is unavailable. The client should also surface diagnostics/metrics and apply circuit‑breaker or fail‑fast behavior to avoid amplifying outages.",
  "source_quote": "Loading servers...\n\nRetry"
}

{
  "answer_text": "Treat the upstream MCP Registry as the canonical authoritative starting point, but assume downstream sub-registries can transform, augment, and lag behind that canonical data; do not assume downstream ordering or freshness matches upstream. Clients must therefore reconcile sources and prefer the most recent authoritative upstream signals when conflicts arise, preserve provenance (which registry authored each entry), and apply updates in a well-defined order (using versioning/timestamps/sequence numbers) rather than blindly trusting a single feed. Implementation measures should include per-entry versioning and provenance metadata, ordered update application (or subscribe to change feeds), live validation of reachability and compatibility before connecting, and honoring upstream removal/denylist signals as authoritative to avoid connecting to stale, removed, or conflicting servers.",
  "source_quote": "Our goal is to standardize how servers are distributed and discovered, providing a primary source of truth that sub-registries can build upon."
}

{
  "answer_text": "Detect removals quickly (periodic refreshes, cache invalidation, or subscribe to denylist feeds/webhooks) and immediately stop using discovery-based access to the denylisted server. Revoke or rotate any issued credentials or API tokens tied to that server, block or quarantine known endpoints, remove or mark the server in UIs, surface alerts to operators and affected end-users, and run audits to determine whether the client previously executed risky operations that require remediation. Maintain logs and evidence of prior interactions for forensics and compliance. Where possible, automate remediation via denylist hooks to accelerate blocking and credential revocation.",
  "source_quote": "Registry maintainers can then denylist these entries and retroactively remove them from public access."
}

{
  "answer_text": "Treat local and remote transports as having different lifecycle and reliability semantics: implement explicit connection establishment/handshake, timeouts, reconnection with backoff, and graceful shutdown for remote transports rather than assuming in-process behavior. Do not rely on FIFO or duplicate-free delivery — make operations idempotent, add unique request IDs and duplicate-detection, and provide retries with backoff and bounded windows. Keep session sequencing at the protocol layer (sequence numbers, acknowledgements or causal sequencing), and add observability and clear error paths so transient failures can be recovered or aborted without leaving inconsistent session state.",
  "source_quote": "Design for differing transport guarantees: do not rely on FIFO or duplicate-free delivery unless the transport explicitly guarantees it. Implement explicit connection establishment/handshake, timeouts, reconnection with backoff, and graceful shutdown for remote transports."
}

{
  "answer_text": "Keeping server and client roles separate enforces clear trust, operational, and deployment boundaries: servers should own access control, auditing, resource normalization, and backend integration while clients focus on orchestration, UI, and agent logic, enabling reuse and least-privilege security. This separation allows independent scaling and upgrades, simpler reasoning about domain logic, and clearer auditing and tenant isolation. Collapsing them into a monolith creates tight coupling, increases blast radius (clients gaining direct access to sensitive stores), complicates deployment, upgrades, CI/CD, scaling and version compatibility, and makes access control, auditing, and tenant isolation harder to implement correctly.",
  "source_quote": "Create MCP servers to expose your data and tools"
}
